      },
      "text": "**Timbre as spectral envelope:** Every timbre description below maps to a Fourier spectrum. Strings: rich, even harmonic series (many partials at comparable amplitudes). Flute: strong fundamental, weak overtones (nearly sinusoidal — hence \"pure\"). Oboe: strong odd harmonics (approaching square wave — hence \"reedy/nasal\"). Clarinet: cylindrical closed bore means it overblows to the 3rd harmonic (not 2nd), producing mainly odd harmonics — the \"hollow\" chalumeau register is the sound of a missing even-harmonic series. When you read \"dark\" below, think \"fewer high-frequency partials\"; \"brilliant\" means \"strong upper partials.\""
    }
